    "Dockerfile": "FROM python:3.12-slim\nWORKDIR /app\n\n# The SDK is a normal pip dependency, pinned in its own requirements-sdk.txt\n# and installed in a separate step (still pre production-PyPI cutover) — see\n# that file's comment for why it's never mixed into the same install as the\n# packages below, which resolve from public PyPI.\nCOPY requirements.txt requirements-sdk.txt ./\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir --no-deps -r requirements-sdk.txt\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\nCOPY . .\n\nRUN groupadd -g 1001 novaos && useradd -r -u 1001 -g 1001 -s /bin/false novaos && \\\n    chown -R novaos:novaos /app\n\nUSER novaos\n\n# NovaOS runs agent containers with a read-only root filesystem.\n# Only /tmp is writable (mounted as emptyDir). Use /tmp for any temp files,\n# caches, or runtime data. Do NOT write to /app or other paths at runtime.\nENV TMPDIR=/tmp\nENV SERVER_PORT=8001\nEXPOSE 8001\n\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"server:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8001\"]\n",
    "README.md": "# {{agent_name}}\n\nPython NovaOS agent scaffolded by `novaos-cli init --lang python`.\n\n## Quick start\n\n```bash\npip install --no-deps -r requirements-sdk.txt\npip install -r requirements.txt\npython server.py\n```\n\n## `manifest.json`'s `protocol` field\n\n`manifest.json` declares `\"protocol\": \"A2A\"`. Despite the name, this is not a\nwire protocol — it is an adapter selector, and `\"A2A\"` picks the SDK adapter\n(this template's `server.py` is built on `AgentBuilder` / `asgi_endpoint()`,\nwhich speaks A2A). Leave it as `\"A2A\"` unless you are deliberately building a\nREST agent instead. `\"ACP\"` is a deprecated legacy alias for the same\nadapter — still accepted for\nolder scaffolded manifests, but new agents should use `\"A2A\"`.\n\n## SDK dependency\n\nThe Python client SDK (`novaos_client_sdk`, published as the `novaos-agent-sdk`\npackage) is a normal pip dependency, pinned in its own `requirements-sdk.txt`\n— no monorepo or manual vendoring required. It's installed in a separate\n`pip install` step from `requirements.txt` on purpose: it currently resolves\nfrom TestPyPI only (pre production-PyPI cutover), and mixing a TestPyPI\npackage into the same install as public-PyPI packages via `--extra-index-url`\nlets pip pick whichever index has the highest version for that name — a\nsame-named package published (or squatted) on public PyPI could silently\nreplace it. `requirements-sdk.txt`'s own `--index-url` line makes that\ninstall look at TestPyPI exclusively, with no such fallback. That install\nalso needs `--no-deps` on the command line (pip's requirements-file format\nhas no such directive): TestPyPI doesn't mirror public PyPI, so it can't\nresolve the SDK's own runtime dependencies (e.g. `httpx`) — those are\ndeclared directly in `requirements.txt` instead, resolving normally from\npublic PyPI.\n",
    "requirements-dev.txt": "# Local development and `novaos-cli test` only — not installed in the Docker image.\npytest==9.0.3\npytest-asyncio==1.3.0\n",
    "requirements-sdk.txt": "# novaos-agent-sdk is currently published to TestPyPI only, pre production-PyPI\n# cutover. Installed via a separate `pip install --no-deps -r requirements-sdk.txt`\n# (see Dockerfile / README), never with --extra-index-url alongside PyPI: pip\n# resolves --extra-index-url packages by picking the highest version across\n# every configured index, so a same-named package published on public PyPI\n# (accidentally or by a name-squatter) could outrank and replace this one.\n# --index-url below is exclusive to this file's install — this invocation\n# only ever considers TestPyPI, with no fallback to public PyPI at all.\n#\n# --no-deps (passed on the pip command line, not in this file — pip's\n# requirements-file format doesn't accept it as a directive) is required\n# alongside that exclusive index: TestPyPI is not a mirror of public PyPI, so\n# it doesn't have novaos-agent-sdk's own runtime dependencies (e.g. httpx)\n# available to resolve, and this install would otherwise fail. Its actual\n# runtime dependencies are declared explicitly in requirements.txt instead,\n# where they resolve normally from public PyPI.\n--index-url {{python_sdk_index_url}}\n{{python_sdk_package}}=={{python_sdk_version}}\n",
